# Trivy Ignore File
# Use this file to suppress false positives in vulnerability and secret scanning
#
# Documentation: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/

# Example entries:
# CVE-2023-12345          # Description of why this is ignored
# secret:generic-api-key  # False positive in test fixtures

# mkcert (v1.4.4) - built with Go 1.18, upstream unmaintained since Feb 2022
# These CVEs are not exploitable in mkcert's context:
CVE-2024-45337  # SSH authorization bypass - mkcert doesn't use SSH
CVE-2023-24538  # html/template backticks - mkcert doesn't serve HTML
CVE-2023-24540  # html/template JS whitespace - mkcert doesn't serve HTML
CVE-2024-24790  # net/netip issue - mkcert uses standard net package

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cosign (v3.1.3) - CVE-2026-56854 in the embedded golang.org/x/crypto v0.53.0
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REVIEW BY: 2026-12-31 (quarterly review — do not renew without re-checking)
#
# Scanned as `usr/local/bin/cosign (gobinary)`: Trivy reads Go module metadata
# compiled into the third-party sigstore release binary we download in
# lib/base/setup.sh. This repo contains no Go code and no go.mod, so there is
# nothing here to bump. COSIGN_VERSION=3.1.3 is already the newest cosign
# release; the fix needs an upstream rebuild against x/crypto >= 0.55.0.
# `ignore-unfixed: true` does not mask it — Trivy marks it `fixed` because
# 0.55.0 exists upstream, even though no cosign release embeds it yet.
#
# Not exploitable here, verified against the binary rather than assumed. The
# advisory (GO-2026-6303) names exactly two symbols, both SSH *server* paths:
#   golang.org/x/crypto/ssh — NewServerConn, connection.serverAuthenticate
# Neither is present in the shipped binary — the Go linker dropped them as
# dead code. Reproduce on either published artifact (amd64 is what CI scans;
# both were checked against the SHA256 pins in lib/base/setup.sh):
#
#   nm /usr/local/bin/cosign | grep -E 'crypto/ssh\.(NewServerConn|.*serverAuthenticate)'   # -> no output
#   nm /usr/local/bin/cosign | grep -c 'golang.org/x/crypto/ssh\.'                          # -> 253
#
# The second command is the discriminator: x/crypto/ssh IS linked, so the empty
# first result is a real absence and not a grep that silently matches nothing.
# The four callbacks the advisory implicates (PasswordCallback,
# KeyboardInteractiveCallback, NoClientAuthCallback, GSSAPIWithMICConfig.
# AllowLogin) are absent too. What cosign links from x/crypto/ssh is public-key
# parsing only — ParseAuthorizedKey, ParsePublicKey, MarshalAuthorizedKey — and
# every call site we have is verify-blob / sign / attest. No SSH server, no
# listening socket.
#
# Caveat for whoever revisits: a bare CVE line here is GLOBAL, not per-binary.
# It covers every cosign in every scanned image, so it is only as sound as the
# set of binaries the evidence above was established against.
#
# Today that set has exactly one member. lib/base/setup.sh installs cosign at
# the tracked COSIGN_VERSION, unconditionally, into /usr/local/bin during the
# `base` stage — and that is the only cosign the repo ships. The docker and
# kubernetes features used to source a helper that dpkg-installed a second,
# separately pinned 3.0.2 .deb, but it ran in the same Docker stage *after*
# setup.sh, behind a `command -v cosign` short-circuit, so it never installed
# anything. That dead path was removed in #935; the features now assert the
# base install is present instead (lib/base/cosign-require.sh).
#
# So the proof above covers every cosign scanned. If a second install path is
# ever reintroduced, this entry silently extends to a binary nobody checked —
# re-run the two nm commands against it, or narrow this entry. Prefer removing
# it over widening it. tests/unit/base/cosign-require.sh guards the single-path
# invariant.
#
# Remove once a cosign release embeds x/crypto >= 0.55.0. Tracked in #932.
CVE-2026-56854  # x/crypto/ssh source-address bypass - SSH server symbols not linked into cosign

# npm dev tool transitive dependencies - not exploitable in container context
# These are dependencies of global npm packages (jest, mocha, clinic, etc.)
CVE-2025-7783   # form-data - transitive dep, container doesn't process untrusted forms
CVE-2019-10744  # lodash prototype pollution - transitive dep in dev tools only
